# S12.1 final review: trusted controller and complete-worktree handles

## Findings

Executing the deployment controller directly from the shared `chatops`
checkout could run changed module code as root before source checks. Separately,
the repository barrier rejected Git processes and handles below quarantined Git
directories, but did not reject a non-Git process retaining a writable file
descriptor elsewhere in either worktree.

## Resolution

Deployment and recovery now require a temporary `root:root` mode `0500`,
single-link controller at a fixed `/etc/tu1nz` path. Its SHA-256 is supplied by
the reviewed Runtime Freeze launch procedure, verified before controller work,
and matched again to the `control_runtime_sha256` binding in the remotely
fetched annotated freeze before the deployment marker. Direct execution from
the shared checkout fails closed.

The existing `/proc` cwd/file-descriptor scanner now covers both entire
canonical worktrees before parent locking, after parent locking, after root
locking, and together with quarantined Git directories after atomic exchange.
The parent/root barrier prevents new `chatops` opens after the clean scan.
